106
00:08:20,310 --> 00:08:23,360
Good afternoon everyone, and
welcome to the Harlan Museum.

107
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:27,360
My name's Ruth Calloway, and
I'm the curator of our new exhibition,

108
00:08:27,535 --> 00:08:29,360
Lines on the Land,

109
00:08:29,485 --> 00:08:32,159
which brings together
five centuries of maps.

110
00:08:32,359 --> 00:08:35,305
Before you go in, I'd like
to tell you a little about

111
00:08:35,306 --> 00:08:39,360
how it came about, and
how the gallery is laid out.

112
00:08:39,361 --> 00:08:42,088
A lot of people assume
the exhibition was planned

113
00:08:42,112 --> 00:08:44,560
to celebrate the museum's
200th anniversary.

114
00:08:44,561 --> 00:08:45,561
It wasn't.

115
00:08:45,909 --> 00:08:47,560
That's not until next year.

116
00:08:47,659 --> 00:08:50,098
And while we did receive
a very generous gift of

117
00:08:50,122 --> 00:08:52,572
sea charts from the
Whitlock family last spring,

118
00:08:52,573 --> 00:08:56,560
those had already been
earmarked for a separate display.

119
00:08:56,609 --> 00:08:59,760
The real starting point
was rather more dramatic.

120
00:09:00,359 --> 00:09:01,360
Three years ago,

121
00:09:01,509 --> 00:09:04,523
builders were renovating the
East Wing, and behind some

122
00:09:04,623 --> 00:09:09,960
old wooden panelling, they found a rolled
up estate map, almost perfectly preserved.

123
00:09:10,659 --> 00:09:14,760
Everything you'll see today grew
out of the questions that map raised.

124
00:09:15,359 --> 00:09:18,760
Now, most map exhibitions
tell the story in date order,

125
00:09:18,810 --> 00:09:22,760
from the oldest to the newest,
and we did consider doing that.

126
00:09:23,109 --> 00:09:27,160
In the end though, we decided to group the
maps according to what they were made for.

127
00:09:27,559 --> 00:09:30,360
So there are maps
for war, maps for trade,

128
00:09:30,509 --> 00:09:32,960
maps for religious
pilgrimage, and so on.

129
00:09:33,309 --> 00:09:37,160
It means a 16th century chart can
hang next to a 20th century one,

130
00:09:37,309 --> 00:09:40,560
which some visitors find
a little disorienting at first.

131
00:09:41,259 --> 00:09:44,160
We'd also hoped to have
material from every continent,

132
00:09:44,259 --> 00:09:48,160
but sadly two of our loans,
from Australia and from Brazil,

133
00:09:48,359 --> 00:09:49,760
fell through at the last minute,

134
00:09:50,009 --> 00:09:53,160
so the collection is more
European than I'd have liked.

135
00:09:53,484 --> 00:09:55,160
Let me mention one map in particular.

136
00:09:55,884 --> 00:09:58,595
The oldest piece in the
show is a map of a river

137
00:09:58,619 --> 00:10:01,172
valley drawn by a monk
called Brother Anselm.

138
00:10:01,909 --> 00:10:05,160
We're in the unusual position
of knowing exactly who made it,

139
00:10:05,484 --> 00:10:07,160
because he wrote his name in the corner.

140
00:10:08,109 --> 00:10:09,160
You'll notice some water stains,

141
00:10:09,161 --> 00:10:13,160
and there's a popular story that
it survived a flood at the abbey.

142
00:10:13,409 --> 00:10:15,828
In fact, the stains came
from a leaking roof

143
00:10:15,852 --> 00:10:18,172
in a Victorian library
much more recently.

144
00:10:19,334 --> 00:10:24,560
What's really exciting is that for decades
it was catalogued as a 15th century map.

145
00:10:24,959 --> 00:10:26,560
When we analysed the ink last year,

146
00:10:27,009 --> 00:10:31,560
it turned out to be nearly 200
years older than anyone had thought.

147
00:10:32,134 --> 00:10:34,560
I'd strongly suggest you pick
up an audio guide at the desk.

148
00:10:34,561 --> 00:10:36,560
It isn't free, I'm afraid.

149
00:10:36,561 --> 00:10:37,560
There's a charge of £3.

150
00:10:37,561 --> 00:10:42,560
We'd planned to include interviews with
descendants of some of the map makers,

151
00:10:42,959 --> 00:10:44,960
but that part hasn't been recorded yet.

152
00:10:45,659 --> 00:10:48,354
What the guide does
brilliantly though is decode

153
00:10:48,378 --> 00:10:50,972
all the little pictures
and signs on the maps,

154
00:10:50,973 --> 00:10:53,960
which most of us
simply can't read today.

155
00:10:54,809 --> 00:10:57,960
Photography is allowed throughout,
but please switch off your flash.

156
00:10:57,961 --> 00:11:00,960
Some of the inks are
extremely sensitive to light,

157
00:11:00,961 --> 00:11:03,960
which is also why the
gallery is kept rather dim.

158
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:08,360
Your eyes will adjust after
a minute or two, I promise.

159
00:11:08,809 --> 00:11:12,360
And a couple of changes since
we first advertised the exhibition.

160
00:11:12,509 --> 00:11:15,360
We originally said you'd have
to book tickets online in advance,

161
00:11:15,361 --> 00:11:18,760
but demand has been
manageable, so you can just turn up.

162
00:11:19,409 --> 00:11:22,160
Children under 12 still pay
a reduced price, by the way.

163
00:11:22,359 --> 00:11:23,760
They don't get in free.

164
00:11:24,059 --> 00:11:27,160
And the late opening, which
was going to be on Thursdays,

165
00:11:27,409 --> 00:11:29,760
will now be on Fridays, until 9.

166
00:11:30,109 --> 00:11:31,760
Before you hear the rest of the talk,

167
00:11:32,309 --> 00:11:35,360
you have some time to
look at questions 16 to 20.

168
00:12:08,609 --> 00:12:11,360
Right, let me explain the layout.

169
00:12:11,410 --> 00:12:13,160
If you look at the plan in your leaflet,

170
00:12:13,209 --> 00:12:16,360
you'll see the entrance at the
bottom, and as you come in,

171
00:12:16,361 --> 00:12:19,160
the ticket desk is
immediately on your right.

172
00:12:19,309 --> 00:12:21,760
Go past the desk and
right into that corner,

173
00:12:22,009 --> 00:12:24,360
and you'll find the Whitlock C-charts.

174
00:12:24,459 --> 00:12:27,760
You might expect them to be along
the right-hand wall by the windows,

175
00:12:27,761 --> 00:12:32,360
but the light would fade the colours, so
they're tucked away in the corner instead.

176
00:12:32,385 --> 00:12:36,560
In the centre of the room,
you can't miss the giant globe.

177
00:12:36,561 --> 00:12:39,360
The interactive map table
was originally going to stand

178
00:12:39,361 --> 00:12:43,760
between the entrance and the globe,
but it caused queues right at the door.

179
00:12:44,009 --> 00:12:45,960
So it's now on the far side of the globe,

180
00:12:45,961 --> 00:12:49,160
between the globe and
the doorway to the shop.

181
00:12:49,559 --> 00:12:52,760
Along the left-hand wall,
there are two large cases.

182
00:12:53,109 --> 00:12:56,160
The one nearest the entrance
holds our Roman roadmaps.

183
00:12:56,609 --> 00:12:58,760
The estate map, the one the builders found,

184
00:12:58,761 --> 00:13:02,160
is in the second case along,
halfway down that wall.

185
00:13:02,859 --> 00:13:06,560
In the top left-hand corner, you'll
see the staircase up to the library.

186
00:13:07,009 --> 00:13:09,760
There's a short film about how
maps were engraved and printed,

187
00:13:10,309 --> 00:13:13,960
and that's shown on a screen just to
the right of the stairs as you face them.

188
00:13:14,309 --> 00:13:17,360
Not underneath them, that
space is only used for storage.

189
00:13:18,609 --> 00:13:21,760
Just to the left of the shop doorway
is where the children's trail begins,

190
00:13:22,384 --> 00:13:24,560
and you'll probably
hear it before you see it.

191
00:13:25,309 --> 00:13:28,368
And halfway along the window
wall, there's a row of benches,

192
00:13:28,369 --> 00:13:30,960
if you need a rest, with
a lovely view of the river.

193
00:13:31,809 --> 00:13:34,360
And finally, our replica printing press.

194
00:13:34,959 --> 00:13:37,160
We'd hoped to put it in the
middle of the window wall,

195
00:13:37,234 --> 00:13:39,960
but the floor there isn't strong
enough to take the weight.

196
00:13:40,209 --> 00:13:43,960
So, it's in the top right-hand corner,
to the right of the shop doorway.

197
00:13:44,359 --> 00:13:46,960
Our volunteers give
demonstrations every hour.

198
00:13:47,559 --> 00:13:51,960
Right, do enjoy the exhibition, and do
come and find me if you have any questions.

199
00:13:52,309 --> 00:13:53,960
That is the end of part two.

