00:17:03,961 --> 00:17:06,960
I couldn't find any
reliable source for that.

224
00:17:06,961 --> 00:17:07,961
Fair enough.

225
00:17:08,309 --> 00:17:09,960
I think I got that off a blog.

226
00:17:10,459 --> 00:17:13,563
So, really it comes down to
the fact that every hectare of

227
00:17:13,587 --> 00:17:16,960
maize grown for plastic is a
hectare that isn't feeding anyone.

228
00:17:16,961 --> 00:17:17,961
Exactly.

229
00:17:18,259 --> 00:17:19,960
That's the argument you should lead with.

230
00:17:19,961 --> 00:17:20,960
Then, sugar cane.

231
00:17:20,961 --> 00:17:24,392
Now, I went in thinking this
was the worst of the lot, because

232
00:17:24,416 --> 00:17:27,960
you hear so much about rainforest
being cleared for plantations.

233
00:17:27,961 --> 00:17:28,960
And?

234
00:17:28,961 --> 00:17:32,960
Well, the more I read, the more
I realised that's mostly a myth.

235
00:17:33,409 --> 00:17:34,960
At least where plastics are concerned.

236
00:17:35,359 --> 00:17:37,801
Most of it's made from
bagasse, the fibre that's

237
00:17:37,825 --> 00:17:39,972
left over once the
sugar's been extracted.

238
00:17:40,209 --> 00:17:41,960
So it doesn't need any extra land at all.

239
00:17:41,961 --> 00:17:44,960
I reckon it gets a lot of stick
that it really doesn't deserve.

240
00:17:44,961 --> 00:17:46,960
I'd go along with that.

241
00:17:47,309 --> 00:17:48,960
And it's cheap to process, which helps.

242
00:17:49,109 --> 00:17:51,960
Seaweed's my favourite, honestly.

243
00:17:52,259 --> 00:17:54,575
I assumed supply would
be the sticking point, since

244
00:17:54,599 --> 00:17:56,967
wild seaweed can only be
harvested at certain times.

245
00:17:56,968 --> 00:17:58,960
But it's farmed now, isn't it?

246
00:17:58,961 --> 00:17:59,961
All year round.

247
00:18:00,159 --> 00:18:00,960
Yeah, exactly.

248
00:18:00,961 --> 00:18:01,960
So that's not an issue.

249
00:18:01,961 --> 00:18:04,960
And it's strong enough for most packaging.

250
00:18:05,359 --> 00:18:09,960
The trouble is, it still costs about four
times as much to make as ordinary plastic.

251
00:18:09,961 --> 00:18:12,960
And until that comes down, no
manufacturer's going to touch it.

252
00:18:12,961 --> 00:18:14,960
Sadly, I think you're right.

253
00:18:15,159 --> 00:18:18,960
Then there's the one made from used
cooking oil, collected from restaurants.

254
00:18:18,961 --> 00:18:19,960
Haha, yes.

255
00:18:19,961 --> 00:18:21,960
I find that one rather appealing.

256
00:18:22,259 --> 00:18:24,960
The oil's a waste product,
so it ought to be cheap.

257
00:18:24,961 --> 00:18:25,961
It is.

258
00:18:26,110 --> 00:18:27,110
The cost's fine.

259
00:18:27,434 --> 00:18:30,960
But the bags they trialled
apparently kept a faint whiff of chips.

260
00:18:31,309 --> 00:18:33,985
Which isn't ideal if you're wrapping,
I don't know, a birthday cake?

261
00:18:34,009 --> 00:18:35,960
No, quite.

262
00:18:35,961 --> 00:18:37,960
Make sure you mention that.

263
00:18:38,159 --> 00:18:41,960
Next is chitosan, which comes
from shrimp and crab shells.

264
00:18:42,259 --> 00:18:43,960
People expect it to smell of fish.

265
00:18:44,159 --> 00:18:44,960
And doesn't it?

266
00:18:44,961 --> 00:18:45,961
No.

267
00:18:46,159 --> 00:18:48,960
It's completely odourless
once it's been processed.

268
00:18:48,961 --> 00:18:49,961
And it's tough.

269
00:18:50,559 --> 00:18:53,960
The problem is that the shells only
come in while the fishing season's open.

270
00:18:54,409 --> 00:18:56,960
So for months on end,
there's nothing to work with.

271
00:18:57,209 --> 00:18:59,960
So the factories would
be sitting idle half a year.

272
00:18:59,961 --> 00:19:02,960
Yes, that's a real limitation.

273
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:05,960
And the last one is potato
peel, from crisp factories.

274
00:19:06,309 --> 00:19:07,960
I'd have thought the
supply there was enormous.

275
00:19:07,961 --> 00:19:08,960
It is.

276
00:19:08,961 --> 00:19:11,960
They run all year, so there's no shortage.

277
00:19:12,159 --> 00:19:13,960
And it isn't expensive either.

278
00:19:14,059 --> 00:19:15,960
But the material just doesn't hold up.

279
00:19:16,309 --> 00:19:17,960
It tears really easily.

280
00:19:18,159 --> 00:19:20,960
So you couldn't use it for anything
heavier than, say, a sandwich wrapper.

281
00:19:20,961 --> 00:19:21,960
That's fair.

282
00:19:21,961 --> 00:19:23,960
Just make that point clearly.

283
00:19:23,985 --> 00:19:26,960
Oh, and one more thing before we move on.

284
00:19:27,759 --> 00:19:31,960
Are you going to explain the difference
between biodegradable and compostable?

285
00:19:32,309 --> 00:19:34,960
Because a lot of your audience
will assume they're the same.

286
00:19:35,209 --> 00:19:36,960
Ah, I hadn't planned to, no.

287
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:42,924
I suppose I could add a slide near the start,
saying that compostable means it breaks

288
00:19:42,948 --> 00:19:46,972
down within a set time, but usually only in
an industrial facility, not a garden heap.

289
00:19:46,973 --> 00:19:51,960
Yes, do. Otherwise, half your
argument won't make sense to them.

290
00:19:52,459 --> 00:19:55,080
Before you hear the
rest of the conversation,

291
00:19:55,104 --> 00:19:57,972
you have some time to
look at questions 27 to 30.

292
00:20:28,659 --> 00:20:33,960
Remind me, what made you pick this topic
in the first place? Was it your summer job?

293
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:37,215
At the packaging firm in
Brisbane? No, I was only

294
00:20:37,239 --> 00:20:40,972
stacking boxes there. I never
saw how anything was made.

295
00:20:41,159 --> 00:20:44,492
And I'd actually put down
ocean plastics as my first choice.

296
00:20:44,516 --> 00:20:47,972
And nobody else had chosen
it, so I could easily have kept it.

297
00:20:48,459 --> 00:20:52,413
It was a documentary about compostable
coffee cups that changed my mind.

298
00:20:52,437 --> 00:20:55,972
I watched it one night and just
couldn't stop thinking about it.

299
00:20:56,059 --> 00:21:00,270
Well, it shows. Now, the slides.
You've got 12 for 15 minutes,

300
00:21:00,294 --> 00:21:03,960
which is about right, so
don't worry about the number.

301
00:21:03,961 --> 00:21:06,960
I was worried I'd crammed
too much writing onto them.

302
00:21:13,234 --> 00:21:17,068
Actually, no. You've been quite disciplined. It's mostly
key words. My concern is the graphs. The labels are tiny

303
00:21:17,092 --> 00:21:21,612
and the colours are almost identical, so from the back of
a lecture theatre, no one will be able to make them out.

304
00:21:22,959 --> 00:21:26,948
Right. I'll redo those. And for the
opening, I was going to hit them with

305
00:21:26,972 --> 00:21:30,972
a statistic, like how many million
tonnes of plastic get made every year.

306
00:21:31,109 --> 00:21:35,960
Everyone does that. And the numbers are so
huge, they don't mean anything to people.

307
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:38,189
Or I could put a
question to the audience.

308
00:21:38,213 --> 00:21:40,972
Like, who here has used a
compostable cup this week?

309
00:21:44,909 --> 00:21:48,344
That can fall rather flat if nobody answers.
What about the thing you told me last week?

310
00:21:48,368 --> 00:21:51,972
When you buried a so-called biodegradable cup
in your garden and dug it up six months later?

311
00:21:51,973 --> 00:21:53,960
And it looked brand new.

312
00:21:53,961 --> 00:21:57,234
Exactly. Start there. It
happened to you, it's

313
00:21:57,258 --> 00:22:00,972
memorable, and it leads
straight into your argument.

314
00:22:01,509 --> 00:22:03,970
Okay. Yeah, I'll do that.
And the conclusion?

315
00:22:03,994 --> 00:22:05,967
I wondered whether
it's a bit short.

316
00:22:05,968 --> 00:22:09,358
Short is no bad thing at the
end. And you haven't brought

317
00:22:09,382 --> 00:22:12,967
in any new material, which is
good, because people often do.

318
00:22:12,968 --> 00:22:16,722
But you never actually tell us
what you think should happen.

319
00:22:16,746 --> 00:22:19,960
You list the pros and
cons, and then you just stop.

320
00:22:19,961 --> 00:22:21,960
So I'm sitting on the fence.

321
00:22:21,961 --> 00:22:26,960
Firmly. Give them a clear recommendation,
even if it's a cautious one.

322
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:30,633
Right. I'll argue that seaweed
deserves the research funding.

323
00:22:30,657 --> 00:22:32,972
That's what I actually
believe anyway.

324
00:22:35,759 --> 00:22:37,758
That is the end of part three.

325
00:22:37,782 --> 00:22:41,972
You now have one minute to
check your answers to part three.

326
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:05,959
I'll be right back.

327
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:05,960
I'll be right back.

328
00:23:05,961 --> 00:23:06,961
I'll be right back.

329
00:23:35,959 --> 00:23:36,960
I'll be right back.

330
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:05,959
Bye.

331
00:24:05,960 --> 00:24:05,960
Bye.

332
00:24:05,961 --> 00:24:06,961
Bye.

333
00:24:58,719 --> 00:24:59,720
Part 4

334
00:25:01,379 --> 00:25:04,620
You will hear a lecturer giving a talk
about the domestication of the horse.

335
00:25:05,509 --> 00:25:09,440
First, you have some time
to look at questions 31 to 40.

336
00:25:42,959 --> 00:25:43,960
Good morning.

337
00:25:55,029 --> 00:25:59,220
Today I want to look at one
of the most consequential

338
00:25:59,244 --> 00:26:05,160
partnerships in human history,
the one between people and horses.

339
00:26:05,209 --> 00:26:07,945
It's a story that has been
substantially rewritten

340
00:26:07,969 --> 00:26:10,332
in the last decade or
so, largely thanks to

341
00:26:10,370 --> 00:26:16,400
genetics, so some of what you'll find in
older textbooks is, frankly, out of date.

342
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:19,150
I'll start with the evidence
that was traditionally

343
00:26:19,174 --> 00:26:24,640
used, then turn to what the DNA tells
us, and finish with how the horse spread.

344
00:26:24,641 --> 00:26:27,060
For a long time, the
strongest candidate for

345
00:26:27,084 --> 00:26:29,572
the first horsekeeping
society was the bowtie

346
00:26:29,709 --> 00:26:36,080
culture, in what is now northern
Kazakhstan, around 3500 BCE.

347
00:26:36,081 --> 00:26:38,920
The bowtie were clearly
obsessed with horses.

348
00:26:39,119 --> 00:26:42,353
At some of their
settlements, horse bones make

349
00:26:42,377 --> 00:26:45,400
up more than 90% of
all the animal remains.

350
00:26:45,401 --> 00:26:50,360
Of course, huge numbers of bones
might simply mean very successful hunting.

351
00:26:50,361 --> 00:26:54,520
So researchers looked for evidence
that the animals were controlled.

352
00:26:54,521 --> 00:26:56,578
The first line of evidence
came not from the

353
00:26:56,602 --> 00:26:58,672
legs or the spine, where
you might expect to

354
00:26:58,673 --> 00:27:02,520
see signs of heavy
work, but from the teeth.

355
00:27:02,521 --> 00:27:05,381
Some bowtie horses had
small areas of wear on their

356
00:27:05,405 --> 00:27:08,047
premolars, which was
interpreted as damage from

357
00:27:08,048 --> 00:27:11,880
a bit, the piece of equipment
placed in a horse's mouth to steer it.

358
00:27:12,989 --> 00:27:14,840
The second line of evidence was chemical.

359
00:27:15,659 --> 00:27:19,080
Researchers analyzed residues
left inside bowtie pottery.

360
00:27:19,849 --> 00:27:24,040
Now, if these people were hunters,
you'd expect fats from horse meat.

361
00:27:24,139 --> 00:27:26,280
And that wasn't what made the headlines.

362
00:27:26,379 --> 00:27:29,640
What was significant was the
presence of fats from mares' milk.

363
00:27:30,399 --> 00:27:33,800
And you can't milk a wild
horse, or at least, not twice.

364
00:27:35,049 --> 00:27:38,600
Thirdly, there's the question of
whether horses were kept in enclosures.

365
00:27:39,419 --> 00:27:43,320
Excavators had found rings of post
holes, but these were ambiguous,

366
00:27:43,419 --> 00:27:45,480
as they could equally
be the remains of houses.

367
00:27:46,329 --> 00:27:48,948
Much more persuasive
were unusually high levels

368
00:27:48,972 --> 00:27:51,487
of nitrogen and
phosphorus in the soil within

369
00:27:51,488 --> 00:27:53,838
certain areas, which
is what you'd get from

370
00:27:53,862 --> 00:27:56,452
large quantities of dung
accumulating in a pen.

371
00:27:57,659 --> 00:27:59,480
So the case for bowtie looked strong.

372
00:28:00,249 --> 00:28:03,928
Then, in 2018, a genetic
study sequenced the DNA

373
00:28:03,952 --> 00:28:07,960
of bowtie horses, and the
results were a real surprise.

374
00:28:08,859 --> 00:28:11,480
They were not the ancestors
of the horses we ride today.

375
00:28:12,299 --> 00:28:15,936
Instead, they were the ancestors
of the Przewalski-Schevalski

376
00:28:15,960 --> 00:28:19,400
horse, the stocky animal
found on the Mongolian steppe.

377
00:28:19,549 --> 00:28:21,320
This had an awkward consequence.

378
00:28:22,239 --> 00:28:27,492
Przewalski's horse had always been celebrated
as the last truly wild horse on earth.

379
00:28:28,379 --> 00:28:30,360
That description can no longer stand.

380
00:28:31,279 --> 00:28:33,872
If its ancestors were
kept by the bowtie,

381
00:28:33,896 --> 00:28:36,692
then Przewalski's
horses are feral, that is,

382
00:28:36,829 --> 00:28:39,571
descended from domesticated
animals that escaped

383
00:28:39,595 --> 00:28:42,292
human control and
returned to life in the wild.

384
00:28:43,339 --> 00:28:48,120
Incidentally, by the late 1960s, they
had disappeared from the wild altogether,

385
00:28:48,269 --> 00:28:52,680
and everyone alive today is descended
from a handful of animals kept in zoos.

386
00:28:53,869 --> 00:28:55,320
That left an obvious question.

387
00:28:56,109 --> 00:29:00,200
If modern domestic horses didn't come
from bowtie, where did they come from?

388
00:29:01,179 --> 00:29:04,357
Over the years, various
regions had been proposed,

389
00:29:04,381 --> 00:29:08,360
including Anatolia, in modern
Turkey, and the Iberian Peninsula.

390
00:29:09,529 --> 00:29:12,825
A very large study
published in 2021, which

391
00:29:12,849 --> 00:29:17,880
analyzed the genomes of hundreds of
ancient horses, finally gave an answer.

392
00:29:18,669 --> 00:29:21,252
The western steppe,
around the lower reaches

393
00:29:21,276 --> 00:29:27,480
of the Volga and Don Rivers, in what is
now southern Russia, at roughly 2200 BCE.

394
00:29:28,829 --> 00:29:31,395
The same study identified
genes that seem to have

395
00:29:31,419 --> 00:29:33,892
been strongly selected
by those early breeders.

396
00:29:34,589 --> 00:29:36,680
You might expect speed
to have been a priority.

397
00:29:37,579 --> 00:29:39,480
It wasn't, at least not at first.

398
00:29:40,379 --> 00:29:43,320
One of the genes is
associated with a stronger back,

399
00:29:43,494 --> 00:29:46,760
which would have made the animals
better able to carry riders and loads.

400
00:29:47,849 --> 00:29:50,120
Another is linked to more docile behaviour.

401
00:29:50,759 --> 00:29:54,120
Wild horses are prey
animals and they panic easily.

402
00:29:54,269 --> 00:29:58,440
An animal that was less fearful, and
therefore less likely to bolt or kick,

403
00:29:58,589 --> 00:30:00,822
would have been far
more valuable to its owners

404
00:30:00,846 --> 00:30:02,852
than one that was
simply bigger or faster.

405
00:30:04,059 --> 00:30:07,480
There's a further clue in how quickly
generations succeeded each other.

406
00:30:08,169 --> 00:30:10,612
Around this time, the
average interval between

407
00:30:10,636 --> 00:30:13,092
a horse's birth and
the birth of its offspring

408
00:30:13,229 --> 00:30:15,400
fell to roughly half its previous length.

409
00:30:16,349 --> 00:30:18,280
This isn't something
that happens naturally.

410
00:30:18,939 --> 00:30:21,554
It only really makes sense
if people were deliberately

411
00:30:21,555 --> 00:30:25,000
controlling breeding, choosing
which animals mated, and when.

412
00:30:26,119 --> 00:30:28,768
Now, the genetic
evidence has also forced us

413
00:30:28,792 --> 00:30:31,332
to revisit the role of
the Yamnaya people,

414
00:30:31,469 --> 00:30:35,240
who spread from the steppe
into Europe from about 3000 BCE.

415
00:30:36,319 --> 00:30:39,800
There's a dramatic image, which
you'll still find in popular books,

416
00:30:39,899 --> 00:30:42,840
of Yamnaya warriors galloping
westwards on horseback.

417
00:30:43,659 --> 00:30:46,319
Unfortunately for that
picture, the horses found at

418
00:30:46,343 --> 00:30:50,120
the relevant sites don't belong to
the modern domestic lineage at all.

419
00:30:51,019 --> 00:30:54,128
What seems to have made
the Yamnaya so mobile

420
00:30:54,152 --> 00:30:57,412
was not riding, but heavy
wagons drawn by oxen,

421
00:30:57,549 --> 00:31:00,280
which allowed whole families
to move with their possessions.

422
00:31:01,069 --> 00:31:06,200
Once the modern domestic horse had emerged,
though, it spread extraordinarily fast,

423
00:31:06,274 --> 00:31:09,202
reaching Anatolia,
Central Europe, and much

424
00:31:09,226 --> 00:31:12,372
of Asia within a few
centuries after 2000 BCE.

425
00:31:13,229 --> 00:31:14,520
What drove that expansion?

426
00:31:15,080 --> 00:31:16,080
Not cavalry.

427
00:31:16,514 --> 00:31:20,040
Mounted warfare on any real
scale comes considerably later.

428
00:31:20,749 --> 00:31:26,040
The key development was the chariot,
a light, fast vehicle with spoked wheels,

429
00:31:26,139 --> 00:31:29,240
which turned the horse into a
weapon of enormous prestige.

430
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:31,280
Lecturer.

431
00:31:31,529 --> 00:31:32,680
So, to sum up.

432
00:31:33,419 --> 00:31:36,264
The bowtie story isn't
wrong, exactly, because

433
00:31:36,288 --> 00:31:39,332
the bowtie clearly did
manage horses in some way.

434
00:31:39,979 --> 00:31:41,830
But they turned out
to be a side branch

435
00:31:41,854 --> 00:31:44,052
rather than the main
trunk of the family tree.

436
00:31:44,829 --> 00:31:47,585
It's a useful reminder that
archaeological evidence

437
00:31:47,609 --> 00:31:50,052
which seems to point
clearly in one direction

438
00:31:50,239 --> 00:31:52,840
can be overturned very
quickly by a new technique.

439
00:31:53,769 --> 00:31:57,160
Next week, we'll look at what happened
when horses reached the Americas.

440
00:31:59,819 --> 00:32:01,400
That is the end of part four.

441
00:32:02,289 --> 00:32:06,120
You now have one minute to
check your answers to part four.

